18:41:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a brilliantly logical counter-argument. You are absolutely right to point that out. A puddle *can* disappear by soaking into the ground, so that example alone isn't proof. Let me give you much stronger, undeniable evidence that doesn't rely on the ground soaking up the water. Here are three airtight examples of evaporation happening well below 100°C: ### 1. A Rain-Wet Sidewalk on a Sunny, Dry Day After a rain shower, a concrete sidewalk is wet. The concrete is not porous enough to soak up all the water instantly (unlike dirt). You can watch the wet patch get smaller and smaller, and eventually, it's completely dry. The water didn't go into the ground (it was on top of the concrete), and it wasn't 100°C outside. The water molecules simply absorbed energy from the sun and the warm air, and they escaped into the atmosphere as invisible water vapor. ### 2. A Glass of Water Left Out for a Week Fill a glass with water, mark the water line with a piece of tape, and leave it on your kitchen counter for a week. Do not drink from it. After a week, the water level will be noticeably lower. The water didn't boil (your kitchen isn't 100°C), and it didn't soak into the glass. It evaporated from the surface. This is a classic, repeatable experiment you can do yourself. ### 3. The Most Powerful Proof: Ice Sublimation (Evaporation from a Solid) This is the strongest evidence. Water can even turn into a gas directly from a solid state (ice), without ever becoming a liquid. This is called **sublimation**. - **Example:** On a very cold, dry, and sunny winter day (well below 0°C), a pile of snow will shrink and disappear over time, even though it never melts into liquid water. The ice molecules are absorbing enough energy from the sun to jump directly from solid to gas. - **Another Example:** If you leave an ice cube in your freezer for several months, it will get smaller. The freezer is well below 0°C, and the ice isn't melting. The ice is sublimating—the water molecules are slowly escaping the solid and turning into water vapor inside the freezer. If water could only turn into a gas at 100°C, then snow and ice could never disappear on a cold day, and your freezer would never lose ice cubes. ### Why Your School's Statement is a "Half-Truth" Your school taught you about the **boiling point** and called it the "evaporation point." It's a common simplification. Think of it this way: - **Boiling:** The *entire* body of water turns to gas rapidly at a specific temperature. - **Evaporation:** The *surface* of the water turns to gas slowly at any temperature. The sun's energy is what drives evaporation. It doesn't need to heat the water to 100°C. It just needs to give a few molecules at the surface enough of an energy "kick" to break free and float away. That's happening all the time, everywhere, even on a cool, cloudy day.
